================================================================================

                        basht -- THEORY OF OPERATION

          bash adapted to the line-multiplexed tagged display model

================================================================================

1. THE PROBLEM AND THE MODEL

In a conventional Unix terminal session, every process that inherits the
terminal writes to it directly. Output from concurrent processes interleaves
at the character level into one merged, unattributed stream, and background
output corrupts the line the user is typing. The terminal multiplexes
nothing; it merely arbitrates by arrival order.

basht implements the line-multiplexed model (per the Univac / OS/Z design,
specified in tsh_spec.md and prototyped in mtsh): no task ever writes to the
real terminal. Every task -- including the shell itself -- writes to
pseudoterminals owned by the shell. The kernel therefore enforces output
attribution: the shell always knows which task produced which bytes, because
they arrive on that task's pty. Output is displayed in whole lines, each
prefixed with an identity tag, and the bottom line of the console is managed
so that completed lines "print through" whatever is being typed.

The result: any number of tasks produce output concurrently while the user
types at a stable prompt, every line on screen says which task it came from,
which process to kill, and where it falls in that task's output.

2. THE TAG

Every displayed line carries the prefix

        [name:n:pid:line]

  name    basename of the first word of the task's command
  n       instance number of that name, counting from 1 per session
          ([hello:2] is the second hello started this session; instance
          counts never reset, so a tag denotes the same task forever)
  pid     process id -- directly kill-able
  line    the task's line counter, from 1, shared across the task's
          stdout, stderr and relayed input, giving the total order of
          that task's console traffic

A mark after n distinguishes streams:  none = stdout,  ! = stderr,
* = shell events about the task,  < = input relayed to the task.
The shell itself is task 0: its own output is tagged [bash:0:pid:line],
and the visible prompt is task 0's pending (bottom) line.

3. ARCHITECTURE

Three new modules, lifted from the mtsh prototype, plus small insertions
at bash's natural choke points:

  basht_filter.c   stage 1: escape stripping; stage 2: line assembly
                   run as a one-line terminal per stream
  basht_display.c  the single writer to the real terminal; bottom-line
                   ownership and print-through
  basht.c          task 0 setup, the capture table, drain loops, the
                   foreground pump, auto-windowing, the window bridge

Insertions:

  shell.c          basht_init() before the interactive reader loop;
                   --basht-bridge dispatch at the top of main()
  jobs.c           make_child(): pty allocation around the one fork
                   point; wait_for(): the pump replaces the blocking
                   wait; give_terminal_to(): neutralized
  parse.y/y.tab.c  after readline accepts a line: display its echo,
                   hand the console to the command
  builtins/feed.def  the `feed' builtin

Footprint (vs. upstream bash-5.3, as of the initial complete
implementation): 11 files, +1887/-21 lines. The division is the
architectural point -- about 1700 of those lines are the new
self-contained modules, while the surgery on bash itself is small
and shallow:

  basht.c                    +1135        new
  basht_display.c             +197        new
  basht_filter.c              +163        new
  builtins/feed.def           +126        new
  basht.h                      +91        new
  parse.y / y.tab.c        +50/-8 each    line endings; accept hook
  jobs.c                    +48/-1        fork, wait, terminal
  shell.c                     +16         init, bridge dispatch
  Makefile.in (both)        +11/-4        wiring

Only 21 lines of existing bash code are deleted. The model attaches
at choke points bash already had -- one fork site, one wait loop,
one parser character source, one readline hook -- which is why the
adaptation stays this contained.

4. TASK 0: THE SHELL IS NOT SPECIAL

At interactive startup, basht_init() dups the real terminal to a private
fd -- from then on the ONLY fd anything writes the screen through -- and
allocates a pty whose slave replaces the shell's own stdout and stderr.
Everything the shell prints (readline's prompt and echo, builtin output,
error messages, job notifications) flows into that pty, through the same
filter as any task's output, and appears as tagged [bash:0] lines. The
prompt the user sees is nothing but task 0's incomplete line, drawn by
the display layer. There is no privileged path from the shell to the
screen; the uniformity is the design.

5. TASK CAPTURE

All job children pass through one fork point, make_child() in jobs.c.
Before the fork, basht allocates three ptys (in, out, err) and records
them in a capture slot; after the fork, the child attaches the slaves as
its default fds 0/1/2. Bash then applies piping and redirections AS
USUAL, which override those defaults -- so the internal legs of a
pipeline, command substitution output, and explicitly redirected streams
are never captured. Only bytes that would have reached the terminal
enter the multiplexer. Command and process substitution forks are
excluded outright (FORK_COMSUB/FORK_PROCSUB); non-interactive shells
never activate any of this.

The out/err ptys exist (rather than pipes) so that isatty() is true in
the child and stdio remains line-buffered. This property is load-bearing;
pipes would batch output into 4KB blocks and destroy liveness.

Children are NOT given a new session and do not own any terminal:
give_terminal_to() is a no-op under basht. Bash's existing process-group
machinery still places each pipeline in its own pgrp, and that is the
signal-delivery handle (section 9).

6. THE FILTER: A ONE-LINE TERMINAL PER STREAM

Each stream owns a line buffer with a cursor, run as a tiny terminal:

  interpreted   \r (cursor to column 0), \b (cursor left), printable
                bytes overwrite at the cursor, CSI K/C/D/G/P/@/X
                (erase-to-end, right, left, column, delete, insert,
                erase-in-place)
  stripped      all other escape sequences (SGR color, OSC titles,
                cursor addressing, modes) and control bytes
  structural    \n completes the line: it is handed to the display and
                the buffer resets; stream EOF flushes a final
                unterminated line so nothing is lost

Filter state lives in the buffer, so sequences split across read()
boundaries never leak fragments. The interpretation subset is exactly
what line-rewriting programs use -- readline's own echo and editing,
progress bars, spinners -- so those reconstruct correctly; everything
two-dimensional is deleted (or triggers windowing, section 10).

Because readline's output is itself routed into task 0's pty, the entry
line is reconstructed from readline's byte stream by this same emulator.
The shell does not tell the display what the prompt looks like; the
display watched it being typed.

7. THE DISPLAY: BOTTOM-LINE OWNERSHIP

The display layer is the only code that writes the real terminal, and it
manages exactly one piece of state: the bottom line, owned by whichever
stream last wrote a character on an incomplete line. When any stream
completes a line:

  1. the bottom line is erased (CR, as many spaces as drawn, CR),
  2. the tagged completed line is written, scrolling the screen,
  3. the owner's partial line is redrawn beneath, undisturbed.

When no stream owns the bottom line, the default owner -- task 0 -- is
drawn: that is the prompt. During a foreground command the default is
suspended (serial semantics: the console belongs to the command), so no
prompt is painted until the shell actually has the keyboard again. The
terminal cursor is parked at the owning stream's cursor column, so
mid-line editing looks right.

8. THE EVENT LOOP

Two phases, neither of which ever blocks the multiplexer:

Prompt phase. readline's character source (rl_getc_function) is replaced
with a select() over the keyboard, task 0's master, and every capture
master. Output drains and displays the moment it lands in a pty; the
keyboard wakes readline per keystroke. (rl_event_hook was rejected: its
wait loop spins forever at EOF on non-tty stdin.) After readline accepts
a line, the accepted line's echo is drained and displayed BEFORE the
command runs, so the transcript reads in causal order.

Foreground phase. wait_for()'s blocking waitpid becomes a pump: select
over the keyboard and all masters, drain and display everything, relay
the keyboard to the foreground task (section 9), reap with WNOHANG.
Background tasks keep printing through while a foreground task runs; the
`wait' builtin and command substitution waits pump too, so nothing
stalls the display.

9. INPUT ROUTING AND SIGNALS

Serial foreground: a command run without & takes the console. The
terminal goes raw; typed characters build the task's pending input line,
displayed as [name:n<] and erased/redrawn through print-throughs like
any partial line; Enter completes it, displays it, and ships it to the
task's in-pty. ^C and ^Z are delivered as killpg(SIGINT/SIGTSTP) on the
task's process group -- terminal ownership never moves, so there is
nothing to take back afterward. ^D writes VEOF. If the task dies
mid-line, the un-shipped characters are pushed into readline's queue as
type-ahead for the next prompt, exactly as a shared terminal would have
preserved them.

Background input: `feed name[:n] text...' writes one line to a task's
stdin without foregrounding it, addressed by tag (the :n may be omitted
when unique; an ambiguous name is an error, not a guess). The name is
`feed' because `in', the tsh spec's name, is a bash reserved word.

10. FULL-SCREEN TASKS: THE WINDOW ESCAPE

basht is for line-oriented commands; a vim is meaningless as lines. When
a captured task enables the alternate screen (CSI ?1049h / ?1047h /
?47h -- the one reliable announcement of a full-screen program, emitted
in its first breath), basht:

  1. stops filtering that task mid-chunk. Its raw prefix buffer (2KB,
     accumulated from byte zero) holds everything it ever wrote,
     trigger included. Detection is only honored inside that window --
     no lines displayed yet, prefix complete -- so nothing is lost.
  2. stops reading its masters. The task keeps writing into the kernel
     pty queue and, if it outruns it, blocks in write(): the backed-up
     pty is the whole flow-control mechanism. No shell-side buffering.
  3. spawns $BASHT_TERMINAL (default "gnome-terminal --") running this
     same binary as `--basht-bridge out-fifo in-fifo'. The bridge goes
     raw, reports its window size (4-byte header), and shuttles bytes.
  4. replays the prefix into the window -- minus terminal queries
     (CSI...n/c), whose late answers would arrive as phantom
     keystrokes -- then shuttles live: task output to the window, gated
     on fifo room so a stalled window backs up into the kernel, window
     keystrokes to the task's stdin.
  5. applies the reported window size to the task's ptys (TIOCSWINSZ)
     and sends SIGWINCH, so the program lays out for the real window.

Closing the window hangs up the task (SIGHUP). A windowed task's
console keyboard relay is permanently released; console typing during
and after the window belongs to the shell. Without a GUI, or if the
spawn fails, basht prints a tagged hint and strips escapes as before.
`clear' and colored output never trigger any of this -- only the
alternate screen does.

11. LINE-ENDING TOLERANCE

Independently of the display model, the parser's character source
(yy_getc) runs the universal line-ending recognizer (after flip.c,
Pascal-P6): any of cr lf, lf cr, bare cr, bare lf is one line ending,
read as a single newline, from every input source -- script files, -c
strings, sourced files, here-documents. Pair state is cleared by any
other character and reset per input stream. CR as data ($'\r', read,
command output) is untouched. DOS and old-Mac encoded scripts run
identically to Unix ones.

12. WHAT IS DEFERRED OR ACCEPTED

  - Window resize after attach is not propagated (size is taken once).
  - Pipeline stdin relay goes to the pipeline's last process.
  - Task instance ids are not bash's [n] job numbers.
  - A task's /dev/tty still names the real terminal (option B signal
    design); programs that insist on /dev/tty for passwords will fight
    the shell for the keyboard.
  - Very long entry lines wrap onto a second screen row and the
    single-row erase cannot clean them.
  - A trap longjmp out of wait_for can leak raw mode (prototype risk).

13. BUILD NOTES

The tree is bash (savannah devel) on branch `basht', configured with
--enable-static-link; the top-level `make basht' recurses and copies the
static binary out as ./basht. The in-tree y.tab.c was generated by
bison >= 3.6 and the grammar does not build with bison 3.5: never let
make regenerate it -- hand-sync any parse.y change into y.tab.c and
touch y.tab.c, y.tab.h and parser-built (also after every configure).
After editing a builtins/*.def, run `make -C builtins <name>.o &&
make -C builtins libbuiltins.a && make'; the top-level make will not.

The mtsh prototype in the parent repository remains the living spec for
display and filter behavior; when in doubt, basht reproduces mtsh's
observable output.

